.TH VERIFY 2
.SH NAME
verify: laplacianorder \- convergence-order verification for pde(2)'s Laplacian
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "verify.m";
verify := load Verify Verify->PATH;

(levels, err) := verify->laplacianorder(8, 5);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Verify
checks that a discretization actually converges at the order it
claims to \(em verification (are we solving the equations right?) in
the numerical-methods sense, not validation (are they the right
equations?), and not just eyeballing that an error got smaller.
.PP
.B Laplacianorder
applies
.IR pde (2)'s
discrete 5-point Laplacian to the known function
.RI sin(2π x )sin(2π y )
on a periodic unit square \(em periodic specifically to avoid any
boundary-treatment order contaminating the measurement, isolating pure
interior-stencil accuracy \(em at
.IR n0 ", 2" n0 ", 4" n0 ", ..."
resolution for
.I levels
levels, and reports, at each level, the max-norm error against the
exact Laplacian and the observed convergence order versus the previous
level (0.0 at the first level, which has none).  A correctly
implemented centred 5-point stencil is 2nd order: order should climb
toward
.I 2.0
as resolution increases, e.g.
.IR 0.000 ", " 1.930 ", " 1.982 ", " 1.996 ", " 1.999
for
.IR n0 =8
over 5 levels.
.PP
The Laplacian is applied via one deliberately small explicit
.B diffuse()
sub-step rather than a new
.IR pde (2)
export: for a step
.I h
small enough that
.B diffuse
takes it unsubdivided,
.RI ( u \fIsub\fRnew\fR - u \fIsub\fRold\fR)/ h
is exactly the discrete Laplacian by construction of the explicit-Euler
update
.B diffuse
performs internally \(em which also keeps this check free of any time-
discretization question, measuring only the spatial operator.
.SH SOURCE
.B /module/verify.m
.br
.B /appl/lib/verify.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR pde (2),
.IR sh-verify (1)
